I enumerated the surface first ([38;5;10mnm -D[0m on the C [38;5;10m.so[0m plus the headers), then worked through every source file. The macro-generated names needed care: [38;5;10mPRIV(foo)[0m → [38;5;10m_pcre2_foo_8[0m, public [38;5;10mpcre2_foo[0m → [38;5;10mpcre2_foo_8[0m, but the [38;5;10mpcre2_compile.h[0m helpers use [38;5;10mPCRE2_SUFFIX[0m [3mwithout[23m a trailing underscore, giving [38;5;10m_pcre2_compile_add_name_to_table8[0m — no underscore before the [38;5;10m8[0m. Width-independent tables like [38;5;10m_pcre2_utf8_table1[0m take no suffix at all.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two things I generated rather than transcribed, to remove transcription risk entirely: all data tables (UCD, chartables, opcode lengths, [38;5;10mutt[0m, and [38;5;10mpcre2_compile.c[0m's file-scope tables) and all 1,147 constants were emitted by C programs compiled against the real headers. That guarantees byte-exact tables and correct values for every [38;5;10m#define[0m.[0m[0m
